Confirmed by reading and replaying. compatible.ts L126-135 (streamOf) does split buffer on \n and treat each `data:` line as its own JSON.parse target -- no field-reassembly before parsing. Repro:

```js
const buffer = 'data: {"choices": [\ndata: {"index":0,"delta":{"content":"ok"},"finish_reason":"stop"}]}\n\ndata: [DONE]\n\n';
for (const line of buffer.split('\n')) {
  if (!line.startsWith('data:')) continue;
  const data = line.slice(5).trim();
  if (data === '[DONE]') continue;
  try { JSON.parse(data); } catch (e) { console.log('THROWS on:', JSON.stringify(data)); }
}
```
Output: both halves throw SyntaxError individually (`{"choices": [` and the trailing half), which per the code's own catch maps to ModelError('invalid_stream') -- so a spec-valid multi-line SSE data field is treated as a transport failure, not reassembled. The byte-chunk fragmentation test in the repo doesn't cover this because it fragments mid-line, not across a logical multi-`data:` field.
